\title{Counting Is Free, Locating Is Hard:\\
Tropical Sealing Theorems for Semiprime Witness Structures}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{13 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We give a unified, fully rigorous account of why five structurally distinct proposals for
integer factorization --- sparse recovery of the divisor indicator, holographic evaluation
of a factoring partition function, ground-state search for a tensor-network parent
Hamiltonian, relaxation in an optical/Ising machine, and the $2$-Sylow torsion census of
$(\ZZ/N\ZZ)^\times$ --- fail for one and the same reason. In each case the object computed
efficiently is a \emph{count}, and each such count is either (i) constant across the class
of inputs, hence information-free; (ii) invariant under a symmetry exchanging the prime
factors, hence unable to distinguish them; or (iii) obtainable only at a cost equal to the
size of the search window it was meant to replace.

The organizing geometry is tropical. In logarithmic coordinates the divisor hyperbola
$xy = N$ is the tropical line $X \odot Y = N$ with corner at $\sqrt N$, and every divisor
pair straddles that corner. The $2$-Sylow torsion census
$T(k) = \#\{x : x^{2^k} = 1 \bmod N\}$ of a semiprime $N = pq$ satisfies
$T(k) = 2^{\min(k,a)+\min(k,b)}$ with $a = v_2(p-1)$, $b = v_2(q-1)$, so its exponent is
\emph{exactly} the min-plus polynomial $(X\oplus a)\odot(X\oplus b)$, whose tropical root
set is $\{a,b\}$. For a squarefree modulus with prime set $S$ the census exponent is the
degree-$|S|$ tropical polynomial $\bigodot_{p\in S}(X \oplus v_2(p-1))$, and the
multiplicity of a tropical root is recovered as a second difference. We prove that the
census, and indeed the entire torsion profile $d \mapsto \#\{x: x^d = 1\}$, is invariant
under \emph{tropical root shuffling}, and exhibit explicit collisions ($21$ vs.\ $77$;
$35$ vs.\ $39$) that seal the whole free-witness family at once. Alongside these we prove
that the divisor-count partition function of a semiprime is the constant $4$; that the
divisor indicator on $[1,\sqrt N]$ is a $2$-spike vector with unique nontrivial spike at
$p$; and that $E(a,b) = (N-ab)^2$ has ground set exactly the four-point divisor set with
spectral gap $1$.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The shape of the question}

Let $N = pq$ be a semiprime with $p < q$ prime. A great deal of exact structural
information about $N$ is available at negligible cost: the divisor lattice of $N$ is a
$2\times2$ Boolean lattice; divisor pairs are symmetric about $\sqrt N$; the group
$(\ZZ/N\ZZ)^\times$ is a product of two cyclic groups whose $d$-torsion has a closed-form
count; and the landscape $E(a,b) = (N-ab)^2$ has an explicit zero set. None of it reveals
$p$. This paper explains why, precisely enough to constitute impossibility proofs for
concrete algorithmic proposals.

We use the following taxonomy, made formal by the theorems below.

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Barrier A (constancy).} A quantity constant on the input class carries zero
information; no post-processing can locate a factor.
\item \textbf{Barrier B (symmetry).} A quantity invariant under a group action mixing $p$
and $q$ cannot distinguish them; two inputs in one orbit with different factorizations
prove impossibility.
\item \textbf{Barrier C (aggregation cost).} A quantity aggregated over a window of size
$W$ costs $\Theta(W)$ unless one already knows where the interesting entry is --- the
original problem.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Notation}

For a prime $\ell$ and $m \geq 1$, $v_\ell(m)$ is the $\ell$-adic valuation; $\tau(N)$ is
the number of divisors; $\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor$ is the integer square root. The
\emph{tropical} (min-plus) semiring is $(\NN\cup\{\infty\},\oplus,\odot)$ with
$x \oplus y = \min(x,y)$ and $x \odot y = x+y$. A one-variable tropical polynomial is a
function $x \mapsto \min_i(c_i + ix)$: concave, piecewise linear, integer slopes. A point
$x_0$ is a \emph{tropical root} (a point of the corner locus) if the minimum is attained by
at least two monomials; the \emph{multiplicity} is the drop in slope across it.

\section{The divisor lattice and the constancy barrier}

\begin{theorem}[Divisor set of a semiprime]\label{thm:divset}
For primes $p,q$ we have $\Div(pq) = \{1,p,q,pq\}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Every divisor of $pq$ factors as $d_1d_2$ with $d_1 \mid p$ and $d_2\mid q$; since
$\Div(p) = \{1,p\}$ and $\Div(q) = \{1,q\}$, the four products $1$, $p$, $q$, $pq$ exhaust
the divisors, and each is realized.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Constant partition function]\label{thm:tau4}
For distinct primes $p \neq q$, $\tau(pq) = 4$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Distinct primes are coprime and $\tau$ is multiplicative on coprime arguments, so
$\tau(pq) = \tau(p)\tau(q) = 2\cdot 2$.
\end{proof}

Model factorization as a counting CSP whose variables are the bits of a candidate divisor
and whose constraint is divisibility. Its partition function with unit weights is the
number of divisor pairs, i.e.\ $\tau(N)$. Holographic (matchgate) algorithms are a
technology for collapsing such sums; Theorem~\ref{thm:tau4} says the collapse here is
total and useless.

\begin{theorem}[No locating from the partition function]\label{thm:tauseal}
There is no $f:\NN\to\NN$ with $f(\tau(pq)) = p$ for all primes $p<q$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply $f$ to $15 = 3\cdot5$ and $35 = 5\cdot7$: both have $\tau = 4$, forcing
$f(4) = 3$ and $f(4) = 5$.
\end{proof}

What a partition-function attack would need is not the normalization $Z$ but the
\emph{marginals} --- the addresses of the witnesses, e.g.\ whether the nontrivial small
divisor is $\equiv 1 \bmod 4$. These depend on $p,q \bmod 4$, and computing them from $N$
alone would already factor $N$: the reduction is circular.

\section{The tropical corner of the divisor hyperbola}

Under $X = \log x$, $Y = \log y$ the hyperbola $xy = N$ becomes the tropical line
$X\odot Y = \log N$, whose corner sits at $X = Y = \tfrac12\log N$, i.e.\ at $\sqrt N$.

\begin{theorem}[Corner straddling]\label{thm:corner}
Let $N \geq 1$ and $d \mid N$. Then
\[
\min\!\left(d, \tfrac{N}{d}\right) \;\leq\; \lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor \;\leq\;
\max\!\left(d, \tfrac{N}{d}\right).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $N = uv$ with $\{u,v\} = \{d, N/d\}$, $u \leq v$. Then $u^2 \leq uv = N$ gives
$u \leq \lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor$, and $N \leq v^2$ gives
$\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor \leq \lfloor\sqrt{v^2}\rfloor = v$.
\end{proof}

Thus the canonical window $[1,\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor]$ is one side of the tropical corner,
and every factorization has exactly one member in it.

\section{Sparsity is real; cheap measurement is not}

Let the \emph{divisor indicator} be $W_N \in \{0,1\}^{\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor}$ with
$W_N(x) = 1$ iff $x \mid N$.

\begin{theorem}[Two-spike structure]\label{thm:twospike}
For primes $p<q$ and $N = pq$,
$\{d \in \Div(N) : d^2 \leq N\} = \{1,p\}$; hence $W_N$ has exactly two nonzero entries.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:divset} the candidates are $1,p,q,pq$. One has $1 \le N$ and
$p^2 < pq = N$; while $q^2 > pq = N$ and $(pq)^2 > pq$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$\#\{d\in\Div(N): d^2 \leq N\} = 2$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Unique nontrivial witness]\label{thm:onespike}
For primes $p<q$ and $N = pq$, $\{d\in\Div(N):d^2\leq N\}\setminus\{1\} = \{p\}$.
\end{theorem}

Recovering the factorization is therefore exactly the location of a single spike in a
binary vector of length $\sqrt N$.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Measurement-cost accounting.}
Compressed sensing recovers an $s$-sparse length-$n$ vector from $m = O(s\log n)$ random
linear measurements; with $s = 2$, $n = \sqrt N$ this is $m = O(\log N)$. But the theory
counts measurements, not operations. A generic measurement vector of length $\sqrt N$
requires $\Theta(\sqrt N)$ bits to specify, and evaluating one inner product with $W_N$
requires $\sqrt N$ divisibility tests. The total, $\Theta(\sqrt N\log N)$, is worse than
trial division. Structured, $N$-computable measurement families avoid the specification
cost but lack the incoherence needed for sparse recovery, and arguing that a given short
probe isolates $p$ requires knowing the residue class of $p$: circular. This is Barrier C
in its sharpest form --- \emph{the measurement specification cost is the aggregation}.

\section{Energy landscapes: a delta, not a slope}

Define $E_N(a,b) := (N - ab)^2 \in \ZZ$, the objective of both tensor-network parent
Hamiltonian and optical/Ising proposals.

\begin{lemma}[Zero set]\label{lem:zeroset}
$E_N(a,b) = 0$ if and only if $ab = N$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Spectral gap / no gradient]\label{thm:gap}
If $ab \neq N$ then $E_N(a,b) \geq 1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$E_N(a,b)$ is a nonnegative integer, and nonzero by Lemma~\ref{lem:zeroset}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Ground space is the divisor set]\label{thm:ground}
For primes $p,q$ and $N = pq$,
\[
\{(a,b) \in \NN^2 : E_N(a,b) = 0\} = \{(1,N),\,(p,q),\,(q,p),\,(N,1)\}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $ab = N$ then $a \mid N$, so $a \in \{1,p,q,N\}$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:divset}, and $b$ is
determined by cancellation. Conversely each listed pair multiplies to $N$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Ground-space cardinality]\label{thm:groundcard}
If additionally $p<q$, the ground space has exactly four elements.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Random-restart density]\label{cor:density}
Over $\{1,\dots,N\}^2$ a uniformly random configuration is a ground state with probability
$4/N^2$; a uniformly random probe in the corner window is a nontrivial witness with
probability $1/\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor$.
\end{corollary}

An optical parametric oscillator network with $L$ modes explores $2^L$ configurations; when
$2^L \approx \sqrt N$ its measured random-restart success rate matches the divisor density
$2/2^L$ (empirically $\approx 0.014$ vs.\ $0.0156$ at $14$ bits, $\approx 0.00025$ vs.\
$0.00024$ at $26$ bits). The mode volume \emph{is} the witness count.

The tensor-network proposal fails complementarily: the target state
$|p\rangle\otimes|q\rangle$ is a product state, of Schmidt rank $1$ and entanglement entropy
exactly $0$, so bond dimension one suffices and the ansatz confers no advantage; the entire
difficulty relocates to variational search over the plateau of Theorems~\ref{thm:gap} and
\ref{thm:ground}.

\section{The torsion census as a tropical polynomial}

For a group $G$ put $\Tor(G,d) := \#\{x \in G : x^d = 1\}$.

\begin{lemma}[Invariance and multiplicativity]\label{lem:torbasic}
$\Tor(-,d)$ is an isomorphism invariant, and $\Tor(G\times H,d) = \Tor(G,d)\Tor(H,d)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Cyclic case]\label{lem:torcyc}
If $G$ is finite cyclic then $\Tor(G,d) = \gcd(|G|,d)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Torsion count of a semiprime modulus]\label{thm:torsemi}
For distinct primes $p,q$ and all $d$,
\[
\Tor\big((\ZZ/pq\ZZ)^\times, d\big) = \gcd(p-1,d)\cdot\gcd(q-1,d).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
CRT gives $(\ZZ/pq\ZZ)^\times \cong (\ZZ/p\ZZ)^\times \times (\ZZ/q\ZZ)^\times$ with cyclic
factors of orders $p-1$, $q-1$; apply Lemmas~\ref{lem:torbasic} and \ref{lem:torcyc}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Squarefree moduli]\label{thm:torprod}
For a finite set $S$ of primes and $N = \prod_{p\in S}p$,
$\Tor((\ZZ/N\ZZ)^\times,d) = \prod_{p\in S}\gcd(p-1,d)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{lemma}[$v_2$ is a tropical morphism]\label{lem:v2}
For nonzero $m,n$: $v_2(\gcd(m,n)) = v_2(m)\oplus v_2(n)$ and
$v_2(mn) = v_2(m)\odot v_2(n)$. Consequently
$\gcd(m,2^k) = 2^{\min(v_2 m,\, k)}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{$2$-Sylow torsion census} of $N$ is $T_N(k) := \Tor((\ZZ/N\ZZ)^\times, 2^k)$, and
for $a,b \in \NN$ the \emph{census exponent} is $\Ee_{a,b}(k) := \min(k,a)+\min(k,b)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Exact census of a semiprime]\label{thm:census}
For distinct primes $p,q$ and all $k$,
\[
T_{pq}(k) = 2^{\,\min(k,a)+\min(k,b)}, \qquad a = v_2(p-1),\quad b = v_2(q-1).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Theorem~\ref{thm:torsemi} with $d = 2^k$, then Lemma~\ref{lem:v2} twice.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Tropicality]\label{thm:trop}
$\Ee_{a,b}(k)$ is the value at $X = k$ of the tropical quadratic
$P_{a,b}(X) = (X\oplus a)\odot(X\oplus b)$. Expanding into monomials,
$\Ee_{a,b}(k) = \min\big(2k,\ \min(a,b)+k,\ a+b\big)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{definition}[Corner locus]
With $M_0(x) = 2x$, $M_1(x) = \min(a,b)+x$, $M_2(x) = a+b$, call $x$ a \emph{tropical root}
of $P_{a,b}$ if $\min_i M_i(x)$ is attained by at least two distinct indices.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Corner locus of the census quadratic]\label{thm:cornerlocus}
$x$ is a tropical root of $P_{a,b}$ if and only if $x = a$ or $x = b$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Assume $a \le b$. At $x=a$, $M_0 = M_1 = 2a \le M_2$; at $x = b$, $M_1 = M_2 = a+b$ minimal.
Conversely a case check on which pair of monomials coincides at the minimum forces
$x \in \{a,b\}$: $M_0 = M_1$ gives $x = \min(a,b)$, $M_1 = M_2$ gives $x = \max(a,b)$, and
$M_0 = M_2$ with minimality forces $a = b = x$.
\end{proof}

So the $2$-adic fingerprint $\{a,b\}$ is exactly the corner locus of the census, which is
concave with slope $2$ below $\min(a,b)$, slope $1$ between the roots, and slope $0$ above.

\begin{theorem}[Slope]\label{thm:slope}
$\Ee_{a,b}(k+1) - \Ee_{a,b}(k) = [k<a] + [k<b]$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The census determines the unordered fingerprint]\label{thm:fingerprint}
If $\Ee_{a,b} = \Ee_{a',b'}$ pointwise then $\{a,b\} = \{a',b'\}$ as multisets.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Explicit census collision]\label{thm:2177}
For all $k$, $T_{21}(k) = T_{77}(k)$, where $21 = 3\cdot7$ and $77 = 7\cdot 11$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$v_2(2) = v_2(6) = v_2(10) = 1$, so both semiprimes have fingerprint $\{1,1\}$ and census
$2^{2\min(k,1)}$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:census}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Census sealing]\label{thm:censusseal}
There is no functional $f$ on census functions with $f(T_{pq}) = p$ for all primes $p<q$.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Higher degree: squarefree moduli}

Let $S$ be a set of odd primes, $N = \prod_{p\in S}p$, and
$\Ee_S(k) := \sum_{p\in S}\min(k, v_2(p-1))$.

\begin{theorem}[Exact census, squarefree modulus]\label{thm:censusmulti}
$T_N(k) = 2^{\Ee_S(k)}$, and $\Ee_S$ is the value function of the degree-$|S|$ tropical
polynomial $\bigodot_{p\in S}\big(X \oplus v_2(p-1)\big)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Slope counts surviving primes]\label{thm:slopemulti}
$\Ee_S(k+1) = \Ee_S(k) + \#\{p\in S : v_2(p-1)>k\}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Tropical root multiplicity]\label{thm:mult}
For all $k$,
\[
\big(\Ee_S(k+1)-\Ee_S(k)\big) - \big(\Ee_S(k+2)-\Ee_S(k+1)\big)
= \#\{p\in S : v_2(p-1) = k+1\}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Newton-polygon duality and concavity]\label{thm:newton}
$\Ee_S(k) = \sum_{j<k}\#\{p \in S : v_2(p-1) > j\}$, and
$\Ee_S(k+2) + \Ee_S(k) \leq 2\Ee_S(k+1)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Level-one census is information-free]\label{thm:level1}
If every $p \in S$ is odd then $T_N(1) = 2^{|S|}$: the first level depends only on the
number of prime factors.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[What the census determines]\label{thm:determines}
If $\Ee_S = \Ee_{S'}$ then for every $k \geq 1$,
$\#\{p\in S: v_2(p-1)=k\} = \#\{p\in S': v_2(p-1)=k\}$.
\end{theorem}

Level $0$ is invisible: a tropical root at $0$ contributes no corner, matching the fact
that the prime $2$ leaves no trace in the $2$-power census.

\section{Root shuffling: sealing the whole free-witness family}

Let $\Pi_N(d) := \Tor((\ZZ/N\ZZ)^\times, d)$ be the full torsion profile.

\begin{theorem}[Tropical root shuffling]\label{thm:shuffle}
Let $m_1,n_1,m_2,n_2$ be nonzero and suppose that for every prime $\ell$ the multisets
$\{v_\ell(m_1),v_\ell(n_1)\}$ and $\{v_\ell(m_2),v_\ell(n_2)\}$ coincide. Then for every
nonzero $d$,
\[
\gcd(m_1,d)\gcd(n_1,d) = \gcd(m_2,d)\gcd(n_2,d).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Two positive integers agree iff their $\ell$-valuations agree for all primes $\ell$. Using
additivity of $v_\ell$ under products and $v_\ell(\gcd(m,d)) = \min(v_\ell m, v_\ell d)$,
the left side has valuation $\min(v_\ell m_1, v_\ell d) + \min(v_\ell n_1, v_\ell d)$, and
this expression is symmetric in the pair $(v_\ell m_1, v_\ell n_1)$; the hypothesis says the
pairs agree up to a swap.
\end{proof}

Tropically: the profile evaluates, at each prime independently, the tropical polynomial
$(X\oplus v_\ell(m))\odot(X\oplus v_\ell(n))$, and a tropical polynomial depends only on
the \emph{multiset} of its roots, never on a labelling.

\begin{theorem}[Explicit shuffle]\label{thm:46}
For every nonzero $d$, $\gcd(4,d)\gcd(6,d) = \gcd(2,d)\gcd(12,d)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Profile collision]\label{thm:3539}
For every $d \geq 1$, $\Pi_{35}(d) = \Pi_{39}(d)$, where $35 = 5\cdot7$ and $39 = 3\cdot13$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:torsemi} the sides are $\gcd(4,d)\gcd(6,d)$ and
$\gcd(2,d)\gcd(12,d)$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:46}, the shuffle exchanging the $2$-adic
valuations $2$ and $1$ between the two slots.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sealing of the free-witness family]\label{thm:fullseal}
There is no functional $f$ on profiles with $f(\Pi_{pq}) = p$ for all primes $p<q$.
\end{theorem}

This rules out in one stroke every algorithm whose only access to $N$ is via the counts
$\#\{x : x^d \equiv 1 \bmod N\}$, for arbitrarily many and adaptively chosen exponents $d$
(an adaptive strategy is a particular functional of the profile). It does not say the
counts are useless: Pollard's $p-1$ method exploits the same arithmetic, but it uses group
\emph{elements} --- an explicit $x$ with $\gcd(x^d-1,N)$ nontrivial --- rather than counts.
That is precisely the counting/locating distinction.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Census evaluation from a known factorization.}
Given $N = \prod_{p\in S}p$, computing $T_N(k)$ for all $k \le K$ costs
$O(|S|(\log N + K))$: get each $v_2(p-1)$ by counting trailing zero bits and accumulate
$\Ee_S(k) = \sum_p \min(k, v_2(p-1))$. Without the factorization, direct enumeration costs
$\Theta(N)$; that asymmetry \emph{is} the sealing.

\paragraph{Fingerprint recovery from the census.}
Given $T(0),\dots,T(K)$ with $\max(a,b) \le K$, set $\Ee(k) = \log_2 T(k)$ and
$s(k) = \Ee(k+1)-\Ee(k) \in \{0,1,2\}$. Then $\min(a,b)$ is the first $k$ where $s$ drops
from $2$ to $1$ and $\max(a,b)$ the first $k$ where $s$ reaches $0$
(Theorem~\ref{thm:slope}); for general squarefree moduli, second differences give root
multiplicities at every level (Theorem~\ref{thm:mult}). Cost $O(K)$.

\paragraph{Corner-window search (the honest baseline).}
Trial division over $[2,\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor]$ finds $p$ in $\Theta(\sqrt N)$ tests and is
guaranteed to succeed by Theorems~\ref{thm:corner} and \ref{thm:twospike}. Every exotic
method analysed here is at best a re-encoding of this baseline.

\paragraph{Collision search.}
To manufacture counterexamples as in Theorems~\ref{thm:2177} and \ref{thm:3539}, enumerate
semiprimes below a bound $B$, key each by the family of local valuation multisets
$\big(\{v_\ell(p-1),v_\ell(q-1)\}\big)_\ell$, and report coinciding keys with different
factorizations; cost $O(B\log B)$ with a sieve. The pairs $(21,77)$ and $(35,39)$ are the
smallest instances.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{One mechanism in five disguises}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}
\toprule
Proposal & Computed object & Failure mode \\
\midrule
Sparse recovery & $O(\log N)$ inner products, length $\sqrt N$ & C: specification cost is the window \\
Holographic partition function & $Z = \tau(N) = 4$ & A: constant across semiprimes \\
Tensor-network Hamiltonian & ground state of $(N-ab)^2$ & A/C: zero entanglement, no gradient \\
Optical/Ising machine & relaxation over $2^L$ modes & A/C: mode volume $=$ witness count \\
Torsion census / profile & $\gcd(p-1,d)\gcd(q-1,d)$ & B: shuffling-invariant; explicit collisions \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The invariant across the table: \emph{the resource changes the physics, not the counting.}

\subsection{Counting versus locating}

The number of divisor pairs is $4$; the number of $d$-torsion units is
$\gcd(p-1,d)\gcd(q-1,d)$; the number of ground states of $(N-ab)^2$ is $4$; the number of
spikes in the window indicator is $2$. Each is a count, and each is constant or symmetric.
Missing in every case is an \emph{address}: which $x$ is the spike, which configuration is
a ground state, which unit realizes a torsion order. The counting versions are trivial and
the search versions are equivalent to factoring --- a strong hint that any successful
attack must be witness-producing, which is exactly the profile of the classical algorithms
that do work.

\subsection{Tropical geometry as the right coordinate system}

Two tropical structures appear. The multiplicative structure of $N$ tropicalizes under
$\log$ to the tropical line with corner at $\sqrt N$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:corner}): the
geometry of the window. The $2$-adic structure of the unit group tropicalizes under $v_2$
to a min-plus polynomial whose roots are the local levels of $p-1$ and $q-1$
(Theorems~\ref{thm:trop}, \ref{thm:cornerlocus}, \ref{thm:censusmulti}): the geometry of
the invariant. The second has teeth, because a one-variable tropical polynomial is
determined by, and determines, only the multiset of its roots with multiplicity. Being
blind to labels is not a defect of the method; it is the definition of a tropical
polynomial.

\subsection{Scope}

Theorems~\ref{thm:tauseal}, \ref{thm:censusseal} and \ref{thm:fullseal} are unconditional
impossibility statements in a restricted access model: an algorithm seeing only the stated
statistic. They say nothing about the complexity of factoring itself. Their value is as
closures: they turn speculative proposals into settled questions and identify exactly which
feature --- constancy, shuffling invariance, or aggregation cost --- any successful proposal
must break.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Tropical Sealing Conjecture]
Let $F$ be any function of $N$ computable from the isomorphism class of $(\ZZ/N\ZZ)^\times$
alone. Then for squarefree $N = \prod p$, $F(N)$ is invariant under any permutation of the
local valuation multisets $\{v_\ell(p-1)\}_{p\mid N}$ preserving the multiset at each prime
$\ell$; consequently, for every $B$ there exist two $B$-bit semiprimes with equal $F$ and
coprime factorizations.
\end{conjecture}

The invariance half follows from Theorem~\ref{thm:shuffle} together with the fact that the
torsion profile is a complete isomorphism invariant of a finite abelian group; the open
part is the density claim --- shuffled pairs at every bit size --- requiring a Linnik or
Bombieri--Vinogradov type input on primes $p \equiv 1 + 2^a u$.

\begin{conjecture}[Tropical Corner Barrier]
Evaluating the census exponent $k \mapsto \min(k,v_2(p-1)) + \min(k,v_2(q-1))$ at a single
$k \in [1,\log N]$ is as hard as factoring: an oracle returning $T(2)$ for arbitrary
semiprimes yields a probabilistic polynomial-time factoring algorithm.
\end{conjecture}

Indeed $T(2) = 2^{\min(2,a)+\min(2,b)}$ distinguishes $a = 1$ from $a \geq 2$, revealing
$p \bmod 4$-type information about the unordered pair; combining this with the classical
square-root-of-unity reduction --- made explicit by the four-point ground space of
Theorem~\ref{thm:ground} --- should upgrade the oracle to a factorization, showing the
sealing of Theorem~\ref{thm:censusseal} is tight rather than an artefact.

\begin{conjecture}[Level-zero invisibility, quantified]
The passage from corner locus to factorization loses exactly the labelling data. Quantify
the fibre: how many $B$-bit semiprimes share a given tropical root multiset, and how that
count grows with $B$. A matching upper bound would show the census leaks
$O(\log\log N)$ bits --- the two level sizes --- and no more.
\end{conjecture}

\section{Conclusion}

Five proposals to factor a semiprime with an exotic resource are each closed by an exact
theorem. The divisor-count partition function is the constant $4$. The divisor indicator on
$[1,\sqrt N]$ is a genuine $2$-spike signal with unique nontrivial spike at $p$, but the
measurements needed to exploit its sparsity cost as much as scanning the window. The energy
$(N-ab)^2$ has ground set exactly $\{(1,N),(p,q),(q,p),(N,1)\}$ with spectral gap $1$ --- a
delta, not a slope --- so both tensor-network and optical relaxations degrade to random
search at the divisor density. And the $2$-Sylow torsion census is exactly
$2^{\min(k,a)+\min(k,b)}$, the tropical quadratic with roots $v_2(p-1)$ and $v_2(q-1)$,
which determines the unordered fingerprint and nothing more, while the full profile is
invariant under shuffling local valuations between the factors and collides on $35$ and
$39$. Tropical polynomials know their roots as a multiset and are blind to labels; counting
problems know cardinalities and are blind to addresses. A method for factoring must, at
some point, produce an address.

\end{document}

